 other investments, that's what you should do,\" Mr Gargash said.\n\nThose other investments continued. Mubadala committed $650 million — with a further $850 million from a multilateral consortium including the World Bank Group's IFC and IDB Group's BID Invest — to begin construction of a sustainable aviation fuel biorefinery in Bahia, Brazil, part of a broader $12.5 billion biofuels ambition there, with production expected from 2029. Core42, G42's sovereign cloud subsidiary, closed $550 million in structured trade finance with HSBC to accelerate AI infrastructure deployments across the US and Europe. Both are executed financings, not term sheets: capital committed, construction beginning.\n\nOne development remains unconfirmed but logged. Deutsche Welle, citing a Middle East Eye report based on two unnamed US officials, reported that Israel and the UAE agreed to establish a joint defense fund for weapons procurement during a covert visit by Israel's prime minister to Abu Dhabi — a visit the UAE has denied occurred. The US Ambassador to Israel separately confirmed that Israel loaned the UAE aerial defense weaponry. If the fund report is ever confirmed, it would represent the most formal institutionalization of a covert defense relationship that has been documented, piecemeal, since April.\n\nA peace deal, if it materializes, would reduce the immediate threat. It would not resolve the Hormuz infrastructure damage timeline, the bilateral rupture with Saudi Arabia, or the shape of the Iran relationship once the guns stop. The UAE has spent this week building the institutional record — in New York, London, Berlin, Luxembourg, Prague — that will matter when those questions come due.",
